  "name": "Question 18",
  "problem": "If $X\u223cB(12,\\frac{2}{3}),$ then, **rounded to $3$ decimal places**, $P(X\\le 11)\u2248${{field-1}}.",
  "worked_solution": "For a binomial random variable $X\u223cB(n,p),$ we have the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X\u223cB(12,\\frac{2}{3}),$ so $X$ has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{12}{x}){(\\frac{2}{3})}^{x}{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{12-x}.\n$$\n\nComputing $P(X\\le 11)$ will require us to compute $f(x)$ at $x=0,1,2,\u2026,11.$ However, if we compute the complement $P(X>11)$ instead, then we will only have to compute $f(x)$ at $x=12,$ which will result in a much faster computation.\n\nComputing $P(X>11),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>11) & =P(X\\in {12}) \\\\  & =f(12) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{12}{12}){(\\frac{2}{3})}^{12}{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{12-12} \\\\  & =1{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{12}{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{0} \\\\  & \u22480.008\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 11) & =1-P(X>11) \\\\  & \u22481-0.008 \\\\  & =0.992\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places.",
